## Keep regular routines

The brain of someone with migraine likes regularity. It can help to:

- **go to bed and get up** at the same time each day, even at weekends
- **eat regular meals** and do not skip breakfast
- **drink plenty of water**
- keep **caffeine** steady rather than having a lot one day and none the next

## Know your triggers

Use a **headache diary** or app to note your attacks, what you were doing, what you ate, your sleep and, if relevant, your periods. Over time you may spot patterns. Try not to avoid lots of things "just in case", as this can make life harder without helping.

## Have a plan for attacks

- carry your **medicine** with you
- take it **early**
- know where you can go to **lie down** in a dark, quiet place
- tell family, friends or colleagues what helps you

## Screens and lighting

- use **night mode** or a screen filter
- take **regular breaks** from screens
- avoid flickering lights where you can
- sunglasses can help outdoors

## Exercise and stress

**Regular, moderate exercise** can reduce attacks for some people. Build up gradually, warm up and drink water. Relaxation, mindfulness and talking therapies can also help, especially if stress is a trigger.

## Support

Migraine can affect your work, relationships and mood. **The Migraine Trust** offers free information and support.
